1
00:00:03,754 --> 00:00:04,754
Let's go. Let's go.

2
00:00:04,839 --> 00:00:05,923
We're gonna be late!

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,940
Adult Sheldon:
Fun fact... the first time

4
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:10,344
I almost died was
at the age of nine.

5
00:00:10,553 --> 00:00:11,553
The murder weapon?

6
00:00:11,596 --> 00:00:13,264
A Jimmy Dean sausage.

7
00:00:18,644 --> 00:00:21,355
Mom, Sheldon's
making faces at me.

8
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:24,358
Sheldon!

9
00:00:24,567 --> 00:00:25,752
Adult Sheldon:
In fairness to Mr. Dean...

10
00:00:25,776 --> 00:00:27,487
- Honey!
- This one morning, I decided

11
00:00:27,695 --> 00:00:30,323
to forgo my normal
20 chews per bite,

12
00:00:30,531 --> 00:00:32,992
as prescribed by the
American medical association.

13
00:00:33,201 --> 00:00:34,201
George, help!

14
00:00:36,496 --> 00:00:39,081
Come here, boy. We got it.
We just got to smack it out.

15
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:40,851
Adult Sheldon:
To further complicate matters,

16
00:00:40,875 --> 00:00:42,585
in any real-life crisis,

17
00:00:42,793 --> 00:00:45,880
my family's default mode
is mindless panic.

18
00:00:46,088 --> 00:00:48,132
Sheldon's gonna die!
Sheldon's gonna die!

19
00:00:48,341 --> 00:00:49,717
Adult Sheldon:
Or heartless apathy.

20
00:00:50,259 --> 00:00:51,636
Mary: Oh, lord.

21
00:00:51,844 --> 00:00:53,164
Here we go.
This is how you do it.

22
00:00:53,346 --> 00:00:55,640
- Here we go. And we shake.
- Honey!

23
00:00:56,057 --> 00:00:58,202
Adult Sheldon: It's interesting
the things you think about

24
00:00:58,226 --> 00:01:00,061
when life is ebbing
from your body.

25
00:01:00,269 --> 00:01:03,314
For instance, linoleum.
What is it, really?

26
00:01:03,523 --> 00:01:06,651
Plastic? And if so, how is it
different from formica?

27
00:01:06,859 --> 00:01:08,194
I'm calling 911.

28
00:01:10,488 --> 00:01:12,208
Adult Sheldon:
And what about count chocula?

29
00:01:12,448 --> 00:01:13,699
How is he a count?

30
00:01:13,908 --> 00:01:15,326
Did the title come with land?

31
00:01:15,535 --> 00:01:16,535
George, heimlich!

32
00:01:18,204 --> 00:01:20,540
Mary:
Don't hurt his little ribs!

33
00:01:20,748 --> 00:01:22,434
Adult Sheldon:
They say, in the final moments,

34
00:01:22,458 --> 00:01:24,794
your life passes
before your eyes.

35
00:01:25,044 --> 00:01:28,130
All I saw was my brother
licking jelly off the knife

36
00:01:28,339 --> 00:01:29,715
and putting it back in the jar.

37
00:01:31,801 --> 00:01:33,219
Okay, it's better now. Sheldon!

38
00:01:33,469 --> 00:01:36,180
Honey. Are you okay?

39
00:01:36,389 --> 00:01:37,807
Can you breathe? Say something!

40
00:01:38,015 --> 00:01:40,810
You have to... throw
away... that jelly.

41
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:46,774
J” nobody else
is stronger than I am j”

42
00:01:46,983 --> 00:01:49,610
j" yesterday I moved a mountain j”

43
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:52,405
j" I bet I could be your hero j”

44
00:01:52,613 --> 00:01:55,032
j" I am a mighty little man j“

45
00:01:55,241 --> 00:01:58,536
j" I am a mighty little man. J“

46
00:02:05,334 --> 00:02:07,503
I've never been late
to school before.

47
00:02:07,712 --> 00:02:09,755
I'm late all the time.
It's no big deal.

48
00:02:09,964 --> 00:02:12,592
I get you to school on time
every day. Why are you late?

49
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:15,219
I have a lot of people
to say hi to in the morning.

50
00:02:15,428 --> 00:02:16,053
Sheldon: Mom,

51
00:02:16,262 --> 00:02:17,462
would you write a note for me?

52
00:02:18,014 --> 00:02:18,806
Sure.

53
00:02:19,015 --> 00:02:20,725
You have the coolest excuse.

54
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:23,519
You almost died.
Did you see Jesus?

55
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:26,022
I saw count chocula.

56
00:02:26,689 --> 00:02:29,692
But feel free to mention
my brush with death in the note.

57
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,278
All right, that's enough
talk about death and dying.

58
00:02:33,362 --> 00:02:36,198
I came this close to
having my own room.

59
00:02:39,452 --> 00:02:40,911
Macelroy:
Remember, the food drive

60
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:41,871
ends Friday.

61
00:02:42,079 --> 00:02:43,748
All canned goods are welcome.

62
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:47,835
But please... make sure
your cans have labels.

63
00:02:48,044 --> 00:02:49,313
Needy people
have enough problems

64
00:02:49,337 --> 00:02:51,672
without having to guess
what's for dinner.

65
00:02:51,881 --> 00:02:54,425
I'm sorry I'm late.
I have a note.

66
00:02:57,303 --> 00:03:00,014
You poor thing, you had
a medical emergency?

67
00:03:00,222 --> 00:03:02,767
Yes, ma'am.
I choked on a sausage.

68
00:03:04,018 --> 00:03:05,144
Boy: How big was it?

69
00:03:05,603 --> 00:03:07,271
About yay big.

70
00:03:11,025 --> 00:03:11,734
Adult Sheldon: To this day,

71
00:03:11,942 --> 00:03:14,445
I still don't understand
why they were laughing.

72
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:22,703
You're sitting in my spot.

73
00:03:23,996 --> 00:03:24,996
Why is it your spot?

74
00:03:25,039 --> 00:03:26,749
It's complicated. Just move.

75
00:03:33,798 --> 00:03:36,884
- You're reading a comic book.
- I am.

76
00:03:37,176 --> 00:03:39,595
You do understand
those are for children.

77
00:03:40,262 --> 00:03:41,262
Have you ever read one?

78
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:44,225
I outgrew picture books
when I was three.

79
00:03:44,433 --> 00:03:45,433
Just eat your lunch.

80
00:04:02,535 --> 00:04:03,535
What's the matter?

81
00:04:04,662 --> 00:04:06,706
I-I don't think I can eat this.

82
00:04:06,914 --> 00:04:08,124
You want to trade?

83
00:04:08,749 --> 00:04:10,209
My mom made me fish soup again.

84
00:04:10,418 --> 00:04:11,978
How long do we have
to be in this country

85
00:04:12,002 --> 00:04:13,546
before I get baloney?

86
00:04:17,007 --> 00:04:19,343
Mm, no, thank you.

87
00:04:19,552 --> 00:04:20,803
I'm not hungry.

88
00:04:21,595 --> 00:04:22,930
"I'm not hungry."

89
00:04:24,223 --> 00:04:26,308
Three words you never hear
in Vietnam.

90
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:29,979
Meemaw: Well, here's
something nice.

91
00:04:30,187 --> 00:04:31,731
You know that Mr. Rosenbloom

92
00:04:31,939 --> 00:04:34,400
with the furniture store
over by the steakhouse?

93
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:37,027
- I don't think so.
- Oh, sure you do.

94
00:04:37,236 --> 00:04:38,779
That Hebrew fella
with the comb-over?

95
00:04:38,988 --> 00:04:40,030
You mean Jewish?

96
00:04:40,322 --> 00:04:42,491
I think they like
to be called Hebrew.

97
00:04:43,284 --> 00:04:44,284
Anyway,

98
00:04:45,286 --> 00:04:46,829
he's asked me to dinner.

99
00:04:47,955 --> 00:04:49,790
Well, good for you.
What'd you say?

100
00:04:50,166 --> 00:04:52,001
I said I'd have
to think about it.

101
00:04:52,209 --> 00:04:53,209
What's to think about?

102
00:04:53,377 --> 00:04:54,920
Well, a lot.

103
00:04:55,129 --> 00:04:57,715
I mean, assuming he enjoys
dinner, he might want

104
00:04:57,923 --> 00:04:59,675
a scoop of meemaw for dessert.

105
00:04:59,884 --> 00:05:01,260
Mom, the kids.

106
00:05:01,886 --> 00:05:03,471
I said "dessert."

107
00:05:03,679 --> 00:05:05,556
Did you know
I was talking about sex?

108
00:05:06,098 --> 00:05:06,807
No.

109
00:05:07,016 --> 00:05:08,267
See?

110
00:05:10,394 --> 00:05:11,520
Why aren't you eating?

111
00:05:12,271 --> 00:05:13,898
I don't think I can.

112
00:05:14,106 --> 00:05:16,442
Sometimes the different foods
touch each other, Sheldon.

113
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:17,735
Not the end of the world.

114
00:05:17,943 --> 00:05:20,321
No, I'm afraid I'll choke again.

115
00:05:20,696 --> 00:05:23,032
Oh, honey, that's
not gonna happen.

116
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,450
Go ahead, eat your dinner.

117
00:05:24,658 --> 00:05:26,118
What's all this about choking?

118
00:05:26,368 --> 00:05:27,912
Sheldon almost died
this morning.

119
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:29,455
- What?!
- Oh, yeah.

120
00:05:29,663 --> 00:05:31,999
Dad was shaking him upside down
like a ketchup bottle.

121
00:05:32,208 --> 00:05:33,501
It was great.

122
00:05:33,751 --> 00:05:35,294
Well, you got to eat
something, baby.

123
00:05:35,503 --> 00:05:36,629
Just have a few bites.

124
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:38,756
I can't. It's too risky.

125
00:05:38,964 --> 00:05:40,216
Dibs on the tater tots.

126
00:05:40,549 --> 00:05:41,989
All right, I'll wrap
that up for you,

127
00:05:42,134 --> 00:05:44,595
and if you change your mind,
you can have some later.

128
00:05:44,804 --> 00:05:46,013
At least drink your milk.

129
00:05:46,847 --> 00:05:47,847
Sure.

130
00:05:48,015 --> 00:05:49,451
Adult Sheldon:
My mother and father saw

131
00:05:49,475 --> 00:05:52,686
that I'd been traumatized and
were patient and understanding

132
00:05:52,895 --> 00:05:55,606
about my fear
of eating solid foods.

133
00:05:56,023 --> 00:05:57,691
This lasted one more day.

134
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:59,294
Mary: Sheldon Lee Cooper,
if you don't

135
00:05:59,318 --> 00:06:01,198
finish that pork chop,
I swear I will chew it up

136
00:06:01,362 --> 00:06:03,042
and spit it in your mouth
like a mama bird.

137
00:06:03,197 --> 00:06:04,197
Do it, mom. Do it.

138
00:06:04,323 --> 00:06:06,635
- Mary, can I speak to you for a second?
- No. Enough is enough.

139
00:06:06,659 --> 00:06:08,285
The boy has got to eat.

140
00:06:08,494 --> 00:06:09,662
Just give me a minute.

141
00:06:12,665 --> 00:06:14,083
Start eating, mister.

142
00:06:17,753 --> 00:06:19,088
You don't mind, right?

143
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:24,301
How about I cut that
pork chop off the bone

144
00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:27,137
and throw it in the
blender with some gravy?

145
00:06:28,764 --> 00:06:31,433
If it fits through
a bendy straw, I'll drink it.

146
00:06:31,642 --> 00:06:32,768
Coming up.

147
00:06:34,478 --> 00:06:36,105
Why does he get a pork shake?

148
00:06:36,313 --> 00:06:37,523
This is all my fault.

149
00:06:37,731 --> 00:06:39,491
I rushed him through
breakfast the other day,

150
00:06:39,525 --> 00:06:41,193
and now look...
He is wasting away.

151
00:06:41,402 --> 00:06:43,320
Oh, come on.
You can't blame yourself.

152
00:06:43,529 --> 00:06:45,715
This isn't gonna last. Remember
the time a bee got in the house

153
00:06:45,739 --> 00:06:47,700
and he slept under a net
for a couple months?

154
00:06:47,908 --> 00:06:49,159
This is different.

155
00:06:49,368 --> 00:06:50,368
- How?
- First of all,

156
00:06:50,536 --> 00:06:51,328
you let that bee in.

157
00:06:51,537 --> 00:06:52,538
The bee was your fault.

158
00:06:52,746 --> 00:06:53,998
- Okay.
- Secondly,

159
00:06:54,206 --> 00:06:55,457
this is about his health.

160
00:06:55,666 --> 00:06:56,750
He is a fragile little boy.

161
00:06:56,959 --> 00:06:58,127
He can't go on like this.

162
00:06:58,335 --> 00:06:59,420
I'm not arguing with you.

163
00:06:59,628 --> 00:07:02,006
Let's just, you know,
give him a little more time.

164
00:07:07,344 --> 00:07:08,554
Now what?

165
00:07:08,846 --> 00:07:10,598
Sounds like your mom's
making margaritas.

166
00:07:14,018 --> 00:07:15,018
What's he drinking?

167
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:16,896
Pork chop and gravy.

168
00:07:17,563 --> 00:07:19,207
I snuck
some broccoli in there, too.

169
00:07:19,231 --> 00:07:20,941
Shh...

170
00:07:41,003 --> 00:07:42,755
I'm real glad
you've been getting out again.

171
00:07:42,963 --> 00:07:44,465
Me, too.

172
00:07:44,673 --> 00:07:47,009
We had a nice dinner,
took a walk.

173
00:07:47,384 --> 00:07:49,053
He's a divorcé, you know.

174
00:07:49,261 --> 00:07:50,262
I didn't.

175
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:52,097
It's not a big deal
for those people.

176
00:07:52,306 --> 00:07:55,267
If they're not a lawyer,
they're related to one.

177
00:07:56,310 --> 00:07:57,537
George sr.: You're actually
cutting the crust off

178
00:07:57,561 --> 00:07:58,881
- before you blend it?
- I left it

179
00:07:59,063 --> 00:08:01,649
on his tuna sandwich yesterday,
he said he could tell.

180
00:08:01,857 --> 00:08:03,108
Only drank half of it.

181
00:08:04,026 --> 00:08:05,786
Mary, you think this has
gone on long enough?

182
00:08:05,986 --> 00:08:06,779
No trouble.

183
00:08:06,987 --> 00:08:09,114
But it'd be better
if I had two blenders.

184
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:10,803
Be better if you stopped
coddling him.

185
00:08:10,908 --> 00:08:12,409
Well, I don't know
what else to do.

186
00:08:12,618 --> 00:08:14,721
I suggested that we take him
to some kind of professional,

187
00:08:14,745 --> 00:08:16,205
but you said he'd outgrow it.

188
00:08:16,413 --> 00:08:17,891
Well, if you're looking
for a psychiatrist,

189
00:08:17,915 --> 00:08:20,709
I bet my new fella's
related to one.

190
00:08:23,545 --> 00:08:24,545
What do you think?

191
00:08:27,174 --> 00:08:28,174
Not sure.

192
00:08:28,968 --> 00:08:30,612
If we take him to a shrink,
it feels like we're admitting

193
00:08:30,636 --> 00:08:31,716
something's wrong with him.

194
00:08:31,887 --> 00:08:34,390
He hasn't had solid food
in five weeks.

195
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:38,394
Well, if there is
something wrong with him,

196
00:08:38,602 --> 00:08:39,853
it ain't constipation.

197
00:08:46,068 --> 00:08:46,694
Okay.

198
00:08:46,902 --> 00:08:48,529
Let's see if we
can find somebody.

199
00:08:48,737 --> 00:08:49,780
Thank you.

200
00:08:50,990 --> 00:08:51,990
Hmm.

201
00:08:57,621 --> 00:08:59,123
Needs a few more cheetos.

202
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:03,252
Adult Sheldon: My mother managed
to locate a family psychiatrist

203
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:04,837
within our price range.

204
00:09:05,212 --> 00:09:06,964
No one seemed to be bothered
that he had

205
00:09:07,172 --> 00:09:09,383
a coupon in the pennysaver.

206
00:09:09,925 --> 00:09:12,678
Is this man gonna make me
eat solid foods?

207
00:09:12,886 --> 00:09:14,763
No, he just wants to
talk to you about it.

208
00:09:14,972 --> 00:09:17,391
Did you tell him I have
a lot to accomplish in my life

209
00:09:17,599 --> 00:09:20,936
and cannot afford to be killed
by an unchewed sausage?

210
00:09:21,770 --> 00:09:23,564
Not in those exact words.

211
00:09:27,526 --> 00:09:28,861
Did you bring the coupon?

212
00:09:30,112 --> 00:09:31,321
Hi. Welcome.

213
00:09:31,530 --> 00:09:32,781
You must be the Cooper family.

214
00:09:32,990 --> 00:09:33,741
We are.

215
00:09:33,949 --> 00:09:35,367
Right on, right on.

216
00:09:35,784 --> 00:09:37,077
This must be Sheldon.

217
00:09:37,411 --> 00:09:38,412
Put 'er there.

218
00:09:39,371 --> 00:09:40,581
No, thank you.

219
00:09:41,498 --> 00:09:42,708
Right on, right on.

220
00:09:42,916 --> 00:09:45,210
Okay, well, here's how
I'd like to start. Mom and dad,

221
00:09:45,419 --> 00:09:47,588
why don't you come talk to me
first, and then, Sheldon,

222
00:09:47,796 --> 00:09:49,798
you and I have
a little rap session.

223
00:09:50,507 --> 00:09:51,507
Folks?

224
00:09:56,513 --> 00:09:57,973
Right on, right on.

225
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:00,642
Feel free to read
any of my comics.

226
00:10:04,605 --> 00:10:06,690
And you guys feel free
to sit on my beanbag!

227
00:10:12,029 --> 00:10:14,656
40 cents? Outrageous.

228
00:10:14,865 --> 00:10:16,305
Adult Sheldon:
As fate would have it,

229
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:19,453
the comic ipicked up
was called X-Men.

230
00:10:19,661 --> 00:10:21,872
Young mutants
with incredible powers

231
00:10:22,081 --> 00:10:24,124
who were feared
and misunderstood

232
00:10:24,333 --> 00:10:26,001
by the entire world.

233
00:10:26,210 --> 00:10:28,462
Hey, it's about me.

234
00:10:29,755 --> 00:10:31,757
Now, the thing
you have to understand is

235
00:10:31,965 --> 00:10:33,634
Shelly is incredibly bright.

236
00:10:33,842 --> 00:10:36,762
I mean, his IQ is right up there
with Albert Einstein

237
00:10:37,012 --> 00:10:39,264
and that English
wheelchair fella.

238
00:10:39,515 --> 00:10:41,308
- Cool.
- He's nine years old

239
00:10:41,517 --> 00:10:44,353
and he's in high school, so you
can't judge him like other kids.

240
00:10:44,812 --> 00:10:45,812
- I hear ya.
- George,

241
00:10:45,896 --> 00:10:47,290
tell him about
how he's always going

242
00:10:47,314 --> 00:10:50,192
on and on about
subatomic particles and such.

243
00:10:50,692 --> 00:10:52,277
Oh, he'll get to gabbin'
about it.

244
00:10:52,486 --> 00:10:55,531
We're accustomed
to his quirks and oddities.

245
00:10:55,739 --> 00:10:59,827
But this whole "being afraid
of solid foods“ thing,

246
00:11:00,244 --> 00:11:01,537
it's got us thrown.

247
00:11:02,287 --> 00:11:04,414
Okay.
Let me just start by saying

248
00:11:04,623 --> 00:11:05,743
you came to the right place.

249
00:11:06,375 --> 00:11:08,627
Sheldon and I happen
to have a lot in common.

250
00:11:08,836 --> 00:11:10,671
I, too, was
a special little boy.

251
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:12,107
In the fourth grade,
I was reading

252
00:11:12,131 --> 00:11:13,465
at a seventh grade level.

253
00:11:14,049 --> 00:11:15,068
And I don't have to tell you
where I was

254
00:11:15,092 --> 00:11:16,092
by the seventh grade.

255
00:11:19,179 --> 00:11:20,389
Tenth.

256
00:11:24,768 --> 00:11:26,770
I'm gonna give
the beanbag a whirl.

257
00:11:30,399 --> 00:11:31,483
What are y'all watching?

258
00:11:31,692 --> 00:11:32,776
Little rascals.

259
00:11:35,487 --> 00:11:37,531
You know most of those kids
are dead, right?

260
00:11:37,739 --> 00:11:38,739
What?

261
00:11:38,866 --> 00:11:41,827
Alfalfa, stymie, buckwheat.

262
00:11:42,035 --> 00:11:43,328
Even petey the dog?

263
00:11:43,537 --> 00:11:45,497
Oh, especially petey the dog.

264
00:11:45,706 --> 00:11:47,207
Thanks for making it depressing.

265
00:11:47,416 --> 00:11:48,584
You're very welcome.

266
00:11:48,792 --> 00:11:50,210
Y'all want to go get
some ice cream?

267
00:11:50,419 --> 00:11:51,211
What about dinner?

268
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:53,172
What about it? Come on.

269
00:11:54,506 --> 00:11:55,674
Is alf dead?

270
00:11:55,966 --> 00:11:58,010
No, but it's just
a matter of time.

271
00:11:58,969 --> 00:12:01,129
You'd be surprised how many
children have food phobias.

272
00:12:01,221 --> 00:12:03,932
- Is that so?
- Oh, yeah. When I was Sheldon's age,

273
00:12:04,141 --> 00:12:05,381
I was afraid of that weird skin

274
00:12:05,434 --> 00:12:06,810
- on top of puddin'.
- Really?

275
00:12:07,019 --> 00:12:08,913
Me, too. You know, you put
a little saran wrap on...

276
00:12:08,937 --> 00:12:09,980
George.

277
00:12:13,025 --> 00:12:14,026
Sheldon?

278
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:15,860
Where the hell did he go?

279
00:12:16,695 --> 00:12:17,695
Sheldon?

280
00:12:18,155 --> 00:12:19,198
Couldn't have gone far.

281
00:12:19,698 --> 00:12:20,698
Why not?

282
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:22,242
Right on, right on.

283
00:12:25,245 --> 00:12:26,389
I checked the bathroom,
the stairs.

284
00:12:26,413 --> 00:12:27,497
He's not in the building.

285
00:12:27,706 --> 00:12:29,266
I'm worried.
No one's answering at home.

286
00:12:29,416 --> 00:12:30,626
Stay calm. We'll find him.

287
00:12:31,126 --> 00:12:31,752
You know,

288
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:32,753
when I was a kid...

289
00:12:32,961 --> 00:12:34,213
Shut up.

290
00:12:34,922 --> 00:12:35,922
Well...

291
00:12:40,302 --> 00:12:42,763
Adult Sheldon: It wasn't typical
for me to wander out into

292
00:12:42,971 --> 00:12:46,600
the urban jungle on my own,
but I was on a mission.

293
00:12:46,808 --> 00:12:50,771
I had gotten my first taste
of serialized superheroes,

294
00:12:50,979 --> 00:12:52,272
and I needed more.

295
00:13:00,197 --> 00:13:03,242
Excuse me,
do you have X-Men #137?

296
00:13:03,450 --> 00:13:06,245
I'd like to know
what happens next, please.

297
00:13:06,745 --> 00:13:08,705
Back bin in the corner.

298
00:13:10,290 --> 00:13:11,625
What are you doing here?

299
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:13,168
Oh, hello, tam.

300
00:13:13,377 --> 00:13:15,462
I thought you said
comic books are for children.

301
00:13:15,754 --> 00:13:17,673
I'm a complicated young man.

302
00:13:21,218 --> 00:13:22,487
I think we should
call the police.

303
00:13:22,511 --> 00:13:24,596
We don't need the police.
We'll find him.

304
00:13:24,805 --> 00:13:26,098
This is all my fault.

305
00:13:26,306 --> 00:13:28,433
He ran away because
we took him to a therapist.

306
00:13:29,142 --> 00:13:30,644
That's $15 we ain't
getting back.

307
00:13:30,852 --> 00:13:32,688
That's what you're
worried about?

308
00:13:33,021 --> 00:13:34,982
No. Course not.

309
00:13:37,526 --> 00:13:39,069
And Sheldon's fine.

310
00:13:39,861 --> 00:13:41,154
You know what he's like.

311
00:13:41,446 --> 00:13:42,197
If someone took him,

312
00:13:42,406 --> 00:13:44,366
I'm sure
they'll bring him right back.

313
00:13:48,620 --> 00:13:50,497
J'j'

314
00:13:55,794 --> 00:13:57,629
I'm enjoying this,
but I'm having trouble

315
00:13:57,838 --> 00:13:59,381
with the onomatopoeia.

316
00:14:01,383 --> 00:14:02,050
The what?

317
00:14:02,259 --> 00:14:03,635
The onomatopoeia.

318
00:14:03,844 --> 00:14:05,721
Words that imitate sounds.

319
00:14:05,929 --> 00:14:08,348
"Kthoom." "snikt."

320
00:14:08,682 --> 00:14:09,682
"Bamf."

321
00:14:09,766 --> 00:14:11,727
Writers shouldn't make up words.

322
00:14:12,311 --> 00:14:14,563
Somebody made up “onomatopoeia."

323
00:14:17,232 --> 00:14:19,901
You challenge me. I like that.

324
00:14:28,410 --> 00:14:30,090
Meemaw, are you gonna have
any more babies?

325
00:14:30,245 --> 00:14:31,830
Good lord, no!

326
00:14:32,039 --> 00:14:33,665
She's too old
to have any more babies.

327
00:14:33,874 --> 00:14:35,834
Don't have to have 'em.
You live long enough,

328
00:14:36,043 --> 00:14:37,723
your hair and your teeth
start falling out,

329
00:14:37,878 --> 00:14:40,172
you start wetting the bed,
you get to be one.

330
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:41,380
That's funny.

331
00:14:41,423 --> 00:14:43,133
We're gonna have to
put a diaper on you.

332
00:14:43,342 --> 00:14:45,427
You won't be laughing
when you're doing it.

333
00:14:45,761 --> 00:14:46,803
I might be.

334
00:14:50,307 --> 00:14:54,061
Hey, meemaw, next to Sheldon,
who's smarter, me or Georgie?

335
00:14:54,269 --> 00:14:55,269
Me, of course.

336
00:14:55,520 --> 00:14:56,271
Why you?

337
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:57,939
My head's bigger.

338
00:14:58,148 --> 00:14:59,608
It doesn't matter.

339
00:14:59,816 --> 00:15:01,860
Tonight is not about Sheldon.

340
00:15:02,569 --> 00:15:05,364
Yeah, right. Everything's
always about Sheldon.

341
00:15:05,572 --> 00:15:06,615
Yeah. Most of the time,

342
00:15:06,823 --> 00:15:08,617
it's like we don't even exist.

343
00:15:09,701 --> 00:15:12,579
I guess Sheldon does get
most of the attention.

344
00:15:12,788 --> 00:15:14,206
Maybe that's a good thing.

345
00:15:15,165 --> 00:15:16,165
How?

346
00:15:16,291 --> 00:15:18,251
Well, if it weren't for him,
your parents would be

347
00:15:18,418 --> 00:15:19,795
on your ass all the time.

348
00:15:20,003 --> 00:15:22,923
Yeah. I guess I never thought
about it that way before.

349
00:15:23,131 --> 00:15:26,218
You kidding me? Right now,
they would be nagging you

350
00:15:26,426 --> 00:15:27,886
about cleaning your room

351
00:15:28,095 --> 00:15:29,513
and doing your homework.

352
00:15:29,721 --> 00:15:32,974
Instead, you're sitting
in a parking lot,

353
00:15:33,183 --> 00:15:35,102
eating a blizzard for dinner.

354
00:15:35,310 --> 00:15:37,562
- With cookie dough in it.
- Exactly.

355
00:15:37,771 --> 00:15:41,650
While Sheldon is stuck sitting
in some boring shrink's office.

356
00:15:41,858 --> 00:15:44,277
Mom told me they were
taking him for a haircut.

357
00:15:45,570 --> 00:15:47,572
Oh. Yeah.

358
00:15:47,781 --> 00:15:49,074
Right.

359
00:15:49,282 --> 00:15:52,953
Well, when he gets home,
say his hair looks good.

360
00:16:02,129 --> 00:16:04,297
J'j'

361
00:16:08,552 --> 00:16:09,552
what?

362
00:16:10,595 --> 00:16:11,638
Nothing.

363
00:16:16,101 --> 00:16:18,478
J'j'

364
00:16:24,359 --> 00:16:25,359
You want one?

365
00:16:26,278 --> 00:16:27,278
No.

366
00:16:28,196 --> 00:16:29,196
Well...

367
00:16:29,322 --> 00:16:31,700
If you change your mind,
help yourself.

368
00:16:34,286 --> 00:16:36,326
Adult Sheldon: [Didn't
have to read many comic books

369
00:16:36,413 --> 00:16:40,333
to understand that
every superhero had a weakness,

370
00:16:40,542 --> 00:16:42,127
something they had to overcome

371
00:16:42,335 --> 00:16:45,464
through an extraordinary act
of courage.

372
00:16:46,590 --> 00:16:49,384
For cyclops,
it was the loss of Jean grey.

373
00:16:49,676 --> 00:16:51,761
For rogue, it was human touch.

374
00:16:51,970 --> 00:16:54,931
For me, it was food
that required chewing.

375
00:16:55,140 --> 00:16:57,976
So if! Truly was a mutant,
I would have to do the same.

376
00:16:58,185 --> 00:17:00,228
J'j'

377
00:17:11,948 --> 00:17:15,410
On this day,
I would not be defeated.

378
00:17:17,829 --> 00:17:19,831
Because this was
the day I became...

379
00:17:21,708 --> 00:17:23,126
The chewer.

380
00:17:24,544 --> 00:17:28,507
Just like that,
I overcame my fear of choking.

381
00:17:28,715 --> 00:17:31,218
All that was left
was my fear of dogs,

382
00:17:31,426 --> 00:17:33,470
birds, insects, germs,

383
00:17:33,678 --> 00:17:35,263
hugging, button fly pants,

384
00:17:35,472 --> 00:17:37,474
rivers, ponds, lakes, oceans,

385
00:17:37,682 --> 00:17:40,310
estuaries, corduroy,
root vegetables,

386
00:17:40,519 --> 00:17:42,395
squeaky balloons,
tinted windows,

387
00:17:42,604 --> 00:17:44,397
take a penny, leave a penny,
fireworks,

388
00:17:44,606 --> 00:17:46,775
potbelly stoves, dust bunnies,

389
00:17:46,983 --> 00:17:49,903
that fuzz on peaches. Ugh.

390
00:17:53,281 --> 00:17:55,825
What I find interesting
is how many supervillains

391
00:17:56,034 --> 00:17:58,745
are scientists: Doctor octopus,

392
00:17:58,995 --> 00:18:01,873
doctor doom, Lex Luthor,

393
00:18:02,082 --> 00:18:04,543
green goblin,
the list goes on and on.

394
00:18:04,918 --> 00:18:05,918
So?

395
00:18:05,961 --> 00:18:07,462
So if the world
doesn't respect me,

396
00:18:07,671 --> 00:18:08,964
I might change sides.

397
00:18:12,801 --> 00:18:14,010
Uh-oh.

398
00:18:14,886 --> 00:18:16,012
Good luck.

399
00:18:23,770 --> 00:18:25,772
Adult Sheldon: I tried
to explain to my parents

400
00:18:25,981 --> 00:18:27,941
that a mutant named cyclops,

401
00:18:28,149 --> 00:18:29,901
who shoots laser beams
out of his eyes,

402
00:18:30,110 --> 00:18:32,112
helped me eat a licorice stick.

403
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:34,155
Went right over their heads.

